      script: `The Dimensions Room is one of the most transformative rooms in the entire palace, so let me explain exactly how it works before we apply it. Here's the concept: every passage in the Bible can be read through five distinct lenses — five dimensions — and most people only ever use one or two. It's like looking at a diamond under five different lights; each angle reveals something the others miss entirely. Let me walk you through each dimension. Dimension one is Literal — what does the text plainly say in its original historical context? Who wrote it, when, to whom, and what did it mean to them? Dimension two is Christ — how does this passage point to, foreshadow, or reveal Jesus Christ? This is the non-negotiable lens of Phototheology: every text must be brought to Christ. Dimension three is Personal, sometimes called "Me" — how does this text speak to my life right now? What is God saying to me personally through these words today? Dimension four is Church — what does this text mean for God's people as a community, as the body of believers across time and geography? And dimension five is Heavenly — how does this text connect to heaven's reality, to the eternal plan, to the world that is to come? Most readers only engage one level — usually literal or personal. They miss three entire dimensions of meaning sitting right there in the same passage. The Dimensions Room trains you to see all five simultaneously, and once you learn to do that, every passage in the Bible becomes five times richer than it was before. Now watch what happens when we apply all five to Psalm 23. Literal: David recalls his real experience as a shepherd and fugitive king, writing from personal encounter with God's tangible provision in a physical land. This is autobiography, not abstraction. Christ: Jesus is the Good Shepherd of John 10 who lays down His life for the sheep — the valley He walked was Calvary itself, and the table He prepares is the communion table stained with His own blood. Personal: I can rest because my Shepherd has already defeated every predator. When anxiety rises, "He maketh me to lie down" is a command I can obey today — not a wish, but a decision to trust. Church: the congregation is the flock — not isolated individuals but a community led together through valleys, fed at the same table, anointed by the same Spirit. No sheep walks alone. Heavenly: "I will dwell in the house of the LORD for ever" — this is the New Jerusalem of Revelation 21, where God Himself is the temple and the Lamb is the light. Psalm 23 ends where Revelation ends: in the eternal presence. One passage, five revelations. And every passage in the Bible carries these same five layers. PhototheologyOS's Dimensions mode in Jeeves walks you through all five for any text you give him.`,
