      title: "Genre Shapes Meaning",
      script: `Connect 6 classifies texts by genre — and this is critical because genre determines how you interpret. Think of it this way: you don't critique a song lyric the way you'd critique a legal contract. They follow different rules. The Bible contains six major genres, and each has its own rules of language. Let me name all six. Prophecy uses symbols, codes, numbers, and timelines. Poetry uses metaphor, rhythm, parallelism, and emotional texture. History tells events, often with theological undertones woven beneath the surface. Gospels are narrative focused on Christ's life, death, and resurrection. Epistles are logical argument and doctrinal explanation, written to specific communities. And Parables are simple stories with hidden theological depth, designed to reveal truth to the seeking heart and conceal it from the casual listener. When you misidentify the genre, you misinterpret the text — like reading poetry as if it were a science textbook, or reading prophecy as if it were a newspaper. Connect 6 protects you from that mistake by training you to identify genre first, then interpret accordingly. Now let's apply it. Psalm 23 is poetry — specifically, a psalm of trust, distinguished from psalms of lament, praise, or imprecation. In a trust psalm, the speaker doesn't ask for deliverance — he declares confidence in a God who has already proven Himself. That's why there are no requests in Psalm 23. It's all declaration. The poetic genre also means it uses parallelism, metaphor, and emotional rhythm. "He maketh me to lie down" is followed by "he leadeth me beside" — action paralleled with action, rest paralleled with movement. You don't read poetry the way you read legal code or historical narrative. When you know the genre, the text opens up properly. Genre is the key that unlocks the right door.`,
      estimatedSeconds: 60,
